// BUG-008 (GAP-Z009 regression): Named functions that use module aliases (e.g. mat::sqrt)
// lost their import_aliases when passed as first-class values to higher-order functions.
// Lambdas did not have this problem because they took a fast path in eval_lambda_call.
//
// Root cause: take_call_state() cleared import_aliases. For named functions passed as HOF
// values, this erased the module context before the function body could use it.
// The saved.import_aliases restoration was insufficient because intermediate HOF calls
// could overwrite the aliases before the named function executed.
//
// Reproduction: define a named function that references a module alias, pass it as a
// value to another function, and invoke it from inside that function.
//
// Expected: named function executes successfully using its captured module aliases
// Actual:   "undefined module alias: 'mat'" (before fix)
//
// Fix: FunctionValue gained field `module_aliases: HashMap<String, PathBuf>` captured
//      at func_def_to_value (definition time). In eval_lambda_call, is_named_fn=true
//      restores import_aliases from the function snapshot, not from the caller.
<# std/math => mat

// Función nombrada que usa un alias de módulo internamente
raiz(x) { <~ mat::sqrt(x) }

// HOF que aplica f a cada elemento de una lista
aplicar(f, lista) { <~ lista$> v -> f(v) }

// Caso 1 — función nombrada pasada directamente como valor a HOF
resultado = aplicar(raiz, [4.0, 9.0, 16.0, 25.0])
>> resultado ¶

// Caso 2 — función nombrada almacenada en variable y luego pasada
fn_ref = raiz
resultado2 = aplicar(fn_ref, [1.0, 100.0])
>> resultado2 ¶

// Caso 3 — función nombrada en pipeline encadenado (anidamiento de HOFs)
duplicar(x) { <~ x * 2.0 }
>> [9.0, 16.0]$> raiz $> duplicar ¶
